    "current_example": {
      "math_academy_id": "e-5393",
      "problem": "Plot the curve defined by the equation $r=4\\sin\\theta$ in polar coordinates.",
      "worked_solution": "The polar equation $r=2a\\sin\\theta$ defines a circle of radius $|a|$ centered at the point $(0,a).$\n\nIn our case, we have $r=4\\sin\\theta,$ and therefore\n\n$$\na = \\dfrac{4}{2} = 2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation $r=4\\sin\\theta$ gives a circle of radius $\\left|2\\right| = 2$ centered at the point $(0,2),$ as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14225098/assets/a6a1f74e065f3508c92f8a438eb1830aeb4f877c355cd7779e86183a0da92183.png)",
